OneiOS take18, userland/metal/patches/metal2vulkan/0005: spare command pools follow their recent use.
Upstream: upstream OneiOS (akshaynexus/OneiOS) 50873fe, userland/metal/m2v-spare-command-pools.patch (2026-11-23), made
for this same stack; applies unchanged (contexts made again here). Its census (m2v_command_pool_census) is exported; no
row of ours reads it yet.
What: a command pool given back counts the most command buffers one command buffer used of it in a window of 10 s
(METAL2VULKAN_POOL_USE_WINDOW_S, 1-3600; 0: off); at the first give-back past the window, a pool that holds more than that
plus 8 is released (its command buffers and their command stream BOs: to Turnip's freed-BO cache, past its budget to the
host) instead of kept. A pool that serves the same command buffers each frame is never released.
Why: the pools in rotation (the newest, taken each frame, and the 8 kept) each kept the command buffers of the largest
command buffer they ever served, each with its last command stream BO: upstream's render server held 2,600-3,400 blobs
under 1 MiB, growing by 200-600 per 10 s an app opened. The board's 0022 trims only pools that stay spare (20 s).
Switch: boot argument oneios-pool-cut=0 (upstream's name; METAL2VULKAN_POOL_USE_WINDOW_S=0). Removable: yes
(ONEIOS_METAL_SKIP=spare-command-pools-follow-use).

